# {{lb|en|transitive}} To place confidence in, to rely on, to confide in.
#: {{ux|en|We cannot '''trust''' anyone who deceives us.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|act=IV|scene=i|passage=If I live to see it, I will never '''trust''' his word after.}}
#* '''October 5, 1751''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''The Rambler'' No. 162
#*: He that '''trusts''' without reserve will at last be deceived.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|with {{m|en|in}}}} To have faith in; to rely on for continuing support or aid.
#: {{ux|en|{{w|In God We Trust|In God We '''Trust'''}}|q=official US motto}}
#* {{quote-song|en|title=w:Trust in Me (The Python's Song)|author=w:Sherman Brothers|year=1967|album={{w|The Jungle Book (soundtrack)|The Jungle Book}}|passage='''Trust''' in me, just in me / Shut your eyes and '''trust''' in me}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give [[credence]] to; to believe; to credit.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|3|2|passage='''Trust''' me, you looke well.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To hope confidently; to believe (usually with a phrase or infinitive clause as the object)
#: {{ux|en|I '''trust''' you have cleaned your room?}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|2 John|1|12|passage=I '''trust''' to come unto you, and speak face to face.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Hebrews|13|18|passage=We '''trust''' we have a good conscience.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} to show confidence in a person by entrusting them with something.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aureng-zebe|passage=Whom, with your power and fortune, sir, you '''trust''', Now to suspect is vain.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To commit, as to one's care; to entrust.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|passage=Merchants were not willing to '''trust''' precious cargoes to any custody but that of a man-of-war.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give credit to; to sell to upon credit, or in confidence of future payment.
#: {{ux|en|Merchants and manufacturers '''trust''' their customers annually with goods.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|followed by {{l|en|to}}}} To rely on (something), as though having trust (on it).
#: {{ux|en|to '''trust''' to luck}}
#: {{ux|en|Having lost the book, he had to '''trust''' to his memory for further details.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To risk; to venture confidently.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=9|passage=[Beguiled] by thee to '''trust''' thee from my side.|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have trust; to be credulous; to be won to confidence; to confide.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|act=II|scene=i|page=236|column=2|passage=More ſhould I queſtion thee, and more I muſt, / Though more to know, could not be more to '''truſt''': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Isaiah|12|2|passage=I will '''trust''' and not be afraid.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To sell or deliver anything in reliance upon a promise of payment; to give credit.

