===Verb===
{{en-verb|+|+|+,tret<l:Northern England,Bristol,colloquial>}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[negotiate]], [[discuss]] terms, [[bargain]] (''for'' or ''with''). {{defdate|from 13th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=w:J. R. R. Tolkien|title=The Return of the King|publisher=George Allen & Unwin
|passage=Now halting a few paces before the Captains of the West he looked them up and down and laughed. 'Is there any in this rout with authority to '''treat''' with me?' he asked.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1985|author=w:Lawrence Durrell|title=Quinx|publisher=Faber & Faber|passage=After all, in this hideous war we have just passed through never forget that Halifax would have '''treated''' with Hitler: it took Churchill to refuse.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=David Mitchell|work=The Observer|date=6 Jun 2010|passage=I wouldn't promote businesses I considered immoral – ambulance-chasing lawyers or online roulette for example – but I've got nothing against computer or software manufacture: they're important and any reputable company in that industry is welcome to '''treat''' for my services.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[discourse]]; to handle a subject in writing or speaking; to conduct a discussion. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|chat|discourse|parley|Thesaurus:converse}}
#: {{ux|en|Cicero's writing '''treats''' mainly of old age and personal duty.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=10|passage=Now of love they '''treat'''.|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1749|author=Henry Fielding| title=The History of Tom Jones, a Foundling| passage=And, indeed, should the excellent Mr Broughton be prevailed on to set fist to paper, and to complete the above-said rudiments, by delivering down the true principles of athletics, I question whether the world will have any cause to lament, that none of the great writers, either antient or modern, have ever '''treated''' about that noble and useful art.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Rhetoric and On Poetics|passage=''Rhetoric'' includes the earliest known theory on the proper use of metaphor; ''On Poetics'' '''treats''' the role of symbolism in drama and poetry.|year=1981|author=w:Aristotle|translator=W. Rhys Roberts; Ingram Bywater|section=A Note on the Illustrations|ol=43202299M|publisher=The Franklin Library|location=Pennsylvania}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To discourse on; to [[represent]] or deal with in a particular way, in writing or speaking. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|bespeak|discuss|talk about|Thesaurus:discuss}}
#: {{ux|en|The article '''treated''' feminism as a quintessentially modern movement.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[entreat]] or [[beseech]] (someone). {{defdate|14th|17th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Only let my family live, I '''treat''' thee.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1515|author=w:Alexander Barclay|title=Eclogues|passage=To '''treate''' a tiran it is but thing in vayne.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To handle, deal with or behave towards in a specific way. {{defdate|from 14th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|behandle|deal with|use}}
#: {{ux|en|You '''treated''' me like a fool.}}
#: {{ux|en|She was tempted to '''treat''' the whole affair as a joke.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To entertain with food or drink, especially at one's own expense; to show hospitality to; to pay for as celebration or reward. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''treated''' my son to some popcorn in the interval.}}
#: {{ux|en|I've done so well this month, I'll '''treat''' you all to dinner (or Dinner is my '''treat'''.)}}
#: {{ux|en|My husband '''treated''' me to a Paris holiday for our anniversary.}}
# {{lb|en|UK politics|law}} To commit the offence of providing food, drink, entertainment or provision to corruptly influence a voter.
# {{senseid|en|apply medical care}}{{lb|en|transitive}} To care for medicinally or surgically; to apply medical care to. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|leech|make better}}
#: {{ux|en|They '''treated''' me for malaria.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Heart: a History|author=Sandeep Jauhar|year=2018|ISBN=9780374168650| page=204| passage=We '''treated''' firefighters suffering from smoke inhalation, giving them oxygen to breathe and albuterol mist to help open their airways.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To subject to a [[chemical]] or other action; to act upon with a specific scientific result in mind. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''treated''' the substance with sulphuric acid.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''treated''' the photo somewhat to make the colours more pronounced.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To provide (someone) with something special and pleasant.
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=04 Dec 76|volume=4|number=23|journal=Gay Community News|page=2|title=Men's Center|text=If good acting in movies is appealing and you enjoy live performances of theatre, you may soon be '''treated''' by the GMC's own endeavors in the theatrical arts.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|title=Chelsea 6-0 Wolves|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19632463
|passage=The Chelsea captain was a virtual spectator as he was '''treated''' to his side's biggest win for almost two years as Stamford Bridge serenaded him with chants of "there's only one England captain," some 48 hours after he announced his retirement from international football.}}

