===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃ed- (bite)}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:tusk|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|tusk|ng=also {{m|enm|tux}}, {{m|enm|tusch}}}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tūsc,tūx||canine tooth, tusk, molar}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*tų̄sk,*tunsk}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*tunþskaz||canine tooth}}, extended form of {{m|gem-pro|*tanþs||tooth}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃dónts|t=tooth}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Tusk||tooth}}, {{cog|fy|tosk||tooth}}, {{cog|is|toskur||a tusk, tooth}} (whence the Old Norse and Icelandic [[w:en:Ratatoskr|Ratatoskr]] and ''[[w:is:Ratatoskur|Ratatoskur]]'' respectively), {{cog|got|𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃||tooth}} and {{m|got|*𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌳𐌹||thorn, tooth}}. {{doublet|en|tush}}. More at {{l|en|tooth}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q185635}} One of a pair of elongated pointed [[tooth|teeth]] (either [[cuspid#Noun|cuspid]]s or [[incisor#Noun|incisor]]s) that extend outside the mouth of an animal such as [[walrus]], [[elephant]] or [[wild boar]], and which continue to grow throughout the animal's life.
#: {{cot|en|fang<id:Q1940269><qq:has different functions>}}
#: {{ux|en|Until the CITES sales ban, elephant '''tusks''' were the 'backbone' of the legal ivory trade.}}
# A small projection on a (tusk) [[tenon]].
# A [[tusk shell]].
# {{lb|en|carpentry}} A projecting member like a [[tenon]], and serving the same or a similar purpose, but composed of several [[step]]s, or [[offset]]s, called ''[[tooth|teeth]]''.<ref>{{R:Webster 1913}}</ref>
# A [[sharp]] [[point]].
# The [[share]] of a [[plough]].

