===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Characterized by [[loud]], [[confused#Adjective|confused]] [[noise#Noun|noise]]. {{defdate|from mid 16th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|noisy|uproarious|Thesaurus:noisy}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Horace|tlr=[[w:Wentworth Dillon, 4th Earl of Roscommon|[Wentworth Dillon, 4th] Earl of Roscommon]]|title=[[w:Ars Poetica (Horace)|Horace: Of the Art of Poetry: A Poem]]|location=London|publisher=Printed and sold by H[enry] Hills,{{nb...|in Black-Fryars near the Water-side.}}|year=1709|page=5|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=ektWAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA3|oclc=1011184311|passage=Rage with Iambicks, arm'd ''[[w:Archilochus|Archilocus]]'' / Numbers for Dialogue and Action fit, / And Favourites of the Dramatick Muſe. / Fierce, Lofty, Rapid, whoſe commanding Sound / Awes the '''tumultuous''' Noiſes of the Pit, / And whoſe peculiar Province is the Stage.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|page=68|passage=The noise in this room was perfectly '''tumultuous''', for there were more children there, than Scrooge in his agitated state of mind could count; and, unlike the celebrated herd in the poem, they were not forty children conducting themselves like one, but every child was conducting itself like forty.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Christmas at Trinity|magazine=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper's New Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]], publishers,{{nb...|327 to 335 Pearl Street, Franklin Square}}|month=February|year=1865|volume=XXX|issue=CLXXVII|page=335|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bPcvAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA335|oclc=924884025|passage=Down showers '''tumultuous''' music from the belfry of Old Trinity— / Merry chiming for His birth, and gave songs for His Divinity!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Howard Philip Rhoades|title=Home Town Homage|magazine=[[w:The Black Cat (magazine)|The Black Cat: Clever Short Stories]]|location=Salem, Mass.|publisher=Shortstory Publishing Company|month=December|year=1917|volume=XXIII|issue=3|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Sj8oAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA1-PA23|oclc=1536525|passage="Flynn! Flynn! Mike Flynn!" came surging a '''tumultuous''' roar from the crowd.}}
# Causing or characterized by [[tumult]]; [[chaotic]], [[disorderly]], [[turbulent]]. {{defdate|from mid 16th c.}}
#: {{synonyms|en|riotous|tempestuous|tumultuary|Thesaurus:disorderly}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=IV|lines=13–18|passage=Yet not rejoycing in his &#91;[[Satan]]'s&#93; ſpeed, though bold, / Far off and fearleſs, nor with cauſe to boaſt, / Begins his dire attempt, with nigh the birth / Now rowling, boiles in his '''tumultuous''' breſt, / And like a devilliſh Engine back recoiles, / Upon himſelf; {{...}}|year=1873}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Samuel Rosewell|title=The Unreasonableness of the Present Riotous and Tumultuous Proceedings: As are Directed against His Majesty King George; and His Faithful Subjects, the Protestant Dissenters:{{nb...|With the Magistrates Conduct upon This Occasion: Consider’d in a Sermon Preach’d July 24, 1715. Being the Next Lord’s-Day after the Bill was Pass’d for Preventing Them. [...] Publish’d at the Request of Several of the Hearers.}}|location=London|publisher=Printed for M. Lawrence,{{nb...|at the Angel in the Poultrey.}}|date=24 July 1715|page=17|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5ePozDxDIM8C&pg=PA17|oclc=863417390|passage=And ſeeing theſe '''Tumultuous''' and Rebellious Men do more immediately vent their Malice on ſome of ''their Fellow-Subjects and Fellow-Protestants'', who ''will be'' true to their Oaths, and their Obligations to the King, whom {{smallcaps|God}} has placed over them, may not one humbly ask, ''As for theſe Sheep, what have they done? What Cauſe is there whereby an Account may be given'' of theſe '''tumultuous''' Inſurrections against them?}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|volume=IV|chapter=V|page=108|passage=The old cavalier stooped his head in token of acquiescence in the command of his Sovereign, but he raised it not again. The '''tumultuous''' agitation of the moment had been too much for spirits which had been long in a state of depression, and health which was much decayed.}}
#* {{RQ:Tarkington Flirt|chapter=5|page=71|passage=She leaned very slowly closer and yet closer to the mirror; a rich colour spread over her; her eyes, gazing into themselves, became dreamy, inexpressibly wistful, cloudily sweet; her breath was '''tumultuous'''.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|year=2007
|author=Laurie Burnham
|title=Rivers
|url=https://archive.org/details/rivers0000burn/
|publisher=w:Chelsea House
|ISBN=978-0-8160-5916-4
|page=43
|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/rivers0000burn/page/43/
|text=A '''tumultuous''' waterway, the Yangtze ranks second after the Amazon in terms of discharge. Fed by copious amounts of melting snow and also heavy rains, the river's flow is 10 times greater than China's Huang Ho (Hwang River, Huanghe; also known in English as the Yellow River).}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Anthony Zurcher|title=Trump Addresses Congress: A Kinder, Gentler President|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-39124349|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180202134157/http://www.bbc.com:80/news/world-us-canada-39124349|archivedate=2 February 2018|work=w:BBC News|date=1 March 2017|passage=In his first address to a joint session of Congress, after a '''tumultuous''' first month in office, Mr [[w:Donald Trump|[Donald] Trump]] delivered a conventional speech in a conventional manner.}}

